1
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The man. The myth. The movie.

2
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Hercules is a animation, family, fantasy film directed by Ron Clements.

3
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Bestowed with superhuman strength, a young mortal named Hercules sets out to
prove himself a hero in the eyes of his father, the great god Zeus.

4
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:30,000
Along with his friends Pegasus, a flying horse, and Phil, a personal trainer, Hercules is tricked by
the hilarious, hotheaded villain Hades, who's plotting to take over Mount Olympus!

5
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:36,000
Hercules (voice), played by Tate Donovan.

6
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:42,000
Young Hercules (voice), played by Josh Keaton.

7
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:48,000
Young Hercules (singing voice), played by Roger Bart.

8
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:54,000
Philoctetes (voice), played by Danny DeVito.

9
00:27:54,000 --> 00:28:00,000
Hades (voice), played by James Woods.

10
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:06,000
Meg (voice), played by Susan Egan.

11
00:34:06,000 --> 00:34:12,000
Hercules is a 1997 American animated musical fantasy comedy film loosely based on the legendary hero
Heracles (known in the film by his Roman name, Hercules), a son of Zeus in Greek mythology.

12
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:18,000
Produced by Walt Disney Feature Animation, the film was directed by John Musker and Ron Clements,
both of whom  co-wrote the screenplay with Donald McEnery, Bob Shaw, and Irene Mecchi.

13
00:40:18,000 --> 00:40:24,000
Featuring the voices of Tate Donovan, Danny DeVito, James Woods, and Susan Egan, the film follows the titular Hercules, a demigod with super-strength raised among
mortals, who must learn to become a true hero in order to earn back his godhood and place in Mount Olympus, while his evil uncle Hades plots his downfall.

14
00:43:24,000 --> 00:43:30,000
Development of Hercules began in 1992 following a pitch adaptation of
the Heracles mythological stories by animator Joe Haidar.

15
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:36,000
Meanwhile, Clements and Musker re-developed their idea for Treasure Planet (2002)
following the critical and commercial success of Aladdin (1992).

16
00:49:36,000 --> 00:49:42,000
Their project was removed from development in 1993, and Musker
and Clements joined Hercules later that same year.

17
00:52:42,000 --> 00:52:48,000
Following an unused treatment by Haidar, Clements and Musker studied multiple interpretations
of Greek mythology before abandoning Zeus's adulterous affair with Alcmene.

18
00:55:48,000 --> 00:55:54,000
The project underwent multiple story treatments and a first script draft was inspired by the
screwball comedy films of the classic Hollywood era and popular culture of the 1990s.

19
00:58:54,000 --> 00:59:00,000
During production, McEnery, Shaw, and Mecchi were brought on
board to revise Musker and Clements' script.

20
01:02:00,000 --> 01:02:06,000
British cartoonist Gerald Scarfe was recruited as production designer and
produced over seven hundred visualization designs of the characters.

21
01:05:06,000 --> 01:05:12,000
Research trips to Greece and Turkey provided
inspiration for the background designs.

22
01:08:12,000 --> 01:08:18,000
Animation for the film was done in California and Paris.

23
01:11:18,000 --> 01:11:24,000
Computer animation was used in several scenes,
predominantly in the Hydra battle sequence.

24
01:14:24,000 --> 01:14:30,000
The production budget was $85 million.

25
01:17:30,000 --> 01:17:36,000
Hercules was released on June 13, 1997, and received generally positive reviews from film critics, with James Woods's performance as
Hades receiving particular praise, but the animation (particularly the visual style) and music received a mixed response.

26
01:20:36,000 --> 01:20:42,000
The film under-performed in its theatrical release notably in comparison to previous animated
Disney films, ultimately earning $252.7 million in box office revenue worldwide.

27
01:23:42,000 --> 01:23:48,000
Hercules was later followed by Hercules: The Animated Series, a syndicated Disney television series focusing on Hercules during his time at the
Prometheus Academy, and the direct-to-video prequel Hercules: Zero to Hero (1999), which consists of four episodes from the TV series.

28
01:26:48,000 --> 01:26:54,000
The film explores themes of peasant, hades, cartoon, villain, musical.

29
01:29:54,000 --> 01:30:00,000
The tone is lighthearted and uplifting and emotional.
